<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarakretagama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1365">1365</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Desawarnana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nagarakretagama, or Desawarnana, is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> poem written in 1365 by the court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-prapanca">Mpu Prapanca</a> in praise of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. It describes the court, royal tours and religious life, and lists nearly a hundred places across the archipelago claimed as Majapahit dependencies, making it a key source for 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama.jpg" alt="Nagarakretagama" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagarakertagama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem is a kakawin, written by a Buddhist cleric under the pen-name Prapañca and dated in its own closing lines to the month of Aśwayuja in Śaka 1287, 1365 CE. It belongs to the puja sastra, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> literature of adoration, and often invokes a divine quality in the king and his family, yet Prapañca seems to have written it incognito after he had retired from the court. He named it Deśavarṇana, Description of Districts. The title Nagarakṛtāgama, Complete Doctrine of the State, appears only in a colophon of one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> line, copied in East Bali in 1740 by a scribe called Artha Pamasah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Cantos 13 and 14 name the lands the king claimed, from Jambi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and Samudra on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> to Barune (Brunei) and Solot (Sulu), Pahang, Kedah and Tumasik, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> now stands, and east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, Makassar, Banda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambon">Ambon</a>, the Onin peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>. The poem describes the capital: a palace ringed by a thick, high wall of red brick, a north gate with doors of decorated iron, courtyards of religious buildings and servants' houses, and beyond them the compound of the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>.</p><p>It tells of the royal walkabout, in which the family visited places of the realm and honoured the king's ancestors, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>'s worship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candi-singhasari">Candi Singhasari</a> for his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>. A seven-day sraddha ceremony for the soul of the Queen Grandmother Gayatri Rajapatni, who had died twelve years before, ends with her deification as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita">Prajnaparamita</a>. Kertanegara is deified as a Shiva-Buddha, joining the gods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> into one Lord of the Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The text survived in palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>. In 1894 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> military expedition destroyed the palace of Mataram-Cakranagara on Lombok and took a manuscript as part of the Lombok treasure. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> philologist J.L.A. Brandes, who accompanied the expedition, is credited with saving the royal library from burning, and his edition of 1902 fixed the title Nagarakṛtāgama in scholarship.</p><p>Kept at Leiden University, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was given back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> during President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s state visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1970 and is held by the National Library of Indonesia. After its translation in the early 20th century the poem became an inspiration for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> movement, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> placed it on the Memory of the World register for Asia and the Pacific in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagarakretagama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A trading state on the northwest coast of Borneo appears in Chinese records from the tenth century under the name Po-ni, which sent missions to the Song court, and in 1365 the Javanese poem <b>Nagarakretagama</b> listed Barune among the dependencies of Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The court's patronage of Hindu-Buddhist traditions produced artistic and literary achievements, including the temples of Panataran and the brick architecture of Trowulan, and Old Javanese works such as the <b>Nagarakretagama</b> of Mpu Prapanca (1365), which describes the realm at its height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…1006 King Mpu Sindok moved the capital of the Mataram kingdom to East Java after a series of volcanic eruptions, and several sources take this as the most likely period of abandonment; Mpu Prapanca's <b>Nagarakretagama</b>, written in the Majapahit era around 1365, still mentions &quot;the vihara in Budur&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/borobudur">Borobudur · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Much of what is known comes from the court poem <b>Nagarakretagama</b> (1365), from inscriptions, and from foreign visitors: Odoric of Pordenone, Ibn Battuta and, in 1413, Ma Huan.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li><li><blockquote>Haru appears in Chinese records from the 13th century and in the Javanese <b>Nagarakretagama</b> of 1365.</blockquote><a href="/state/aru-kingdom">Aru Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Dompo is listed in the Javanese <b>Nagarakretagama</b> among the dependencies of Majapahit, and an expedition to it is recorded in the mid-14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dompu-sultanate">Dompu Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
